<h2>The rules of thumb behind the estimate</h2>
<p class="lead">Designers do not light by formula, but there are reliable starting points. This estimator uses common spacing guidelines to show roughly how many fixtures each part of a property tends to need.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Rule of thumb</th><th>Typical LED watts</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Facade</td><td>About one uplight or wash per 8–12 ft of elevation</td><td>4–8 W</td></tr>
<tr><td>Columns &amp; entry</td><td>One grazing light per column or feature</td><td>3–5 W</td></tr>
<tr><td>Walkways</td><td>One path light every 8–10 ft, often staggered</td><td>2–4 W</td></tr>
<tr><td>Driveways</td><td>Wider spacing, 12–16 ft, or markers</td><td>2–4 W</td></tr>
<tr><td>Large trees</td><td>Two to three uplights, more for wide canopies</td><td>6–12 W</td></tr>
<tr><td>Small trees &amp; palms</td><td>One or two accents</td><td>3–6 W</td></tr>
<tr><td>Patios</td><td>Roughly one fixture per 60–100 sq ft of living area</td><td>2–4 W</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>The "style" setting scales everything: subtle designs use fewer fixtures and more darkness; showcase designs add layers like cross-lighting on trees and wall washing.</p>
<div class="callout"><strong>Fewer can be better.</strong> A fixture with the right beam spread and position can replace two poorly placed ones. That is why the estimate is a starting point, and why Spring Landscape Lighting always finishes with a night-time adjustment.</div>
<p>Next steps: check the <a href="/tools/transformer-sizing-calculator/">transformer sizing</a> for your total load, see the running cost in the <a href="/tools/energy-cost-calculator/">energy calculator</a>, or read our guides to <a href="/path-light-spacing/">path light spacing</a> and <a href="/tree-uplighting-guide/">uplighting trees</a>. For budget context, see <a href="/landscape-lighting-cost-spring-tx/">what drives landscape lighting cost in Spring, TX</a>.</p>
